            <ArticleTitle>Relief of postlaminectomy syndrome in selected patients by injection of autogenous cerebrospinal fluid.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>274-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We have been using the differential spinal block to study patients referred to our Pain Clinic for treatment of their chronic pain syndromes. In a selected group of chronic-pain patients from whom cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) could not be obtained at the L4 or L5 interspace, administration of autogenous CSF obtained at a higher interspace gave immediate and complete but temporary pain relief. Endorphins were present in these autogenous CSF samples. These studies, indicating that CSF even from chronic-pain patients contains analgesic substances (possibly endorphins), may point to new treatment modalities for certain chronic back pain syndromes.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
